<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <section xml:id="sec-section-1-1">
      <title>Section 01 - Introduction to Plane Vectors</title>

<objectives>
  <p><em>Learning Objectives</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Describe a plane vector using correct notation.</li>
    <li>
      Perform the basic vector operations of scalar multiplication and
      vector addition and subtraction.
    </li>
    <li>Determine whether two vectors are equivalent.</li>
  </ul>
</objectives>

      <subsection xml:id="sec-01-01-notation">
        <title>Describe a Plane Vector Using Correct Notation</title>
  <p>
    The term <em>vector</em> is used by scientists to describe a quantity
    (such as displacement, velocity, or force) that has both magnitude
    and direction. A vector is often represented by an arrow, or directed
    line segment. The length of the arrow represents the magnitude of the
    vector, and the arrow points in the direction of the vector.
  </p>

  <p>
    We denote a vector by printing a letter in boldface,
    <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, or by placing an arrow above the letter,
    <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, suppose a particle moves along a line segment from point
    <m>A</m> to point <m>B</m>. The corresponding <em>displacement vector</em>
    <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, shown in <xref ref="figure1"/>,
    has initial point <m>A</m> (the tail) and terminal point <m>B</m>
    (the tip). We indicate this by writing
    <m>\mathbf{v} = \overrightarrow{AB}</m>.
  </p>

<figure xml:id="figure1">
  <image source="images/ch01/Figure1.png">
    <shortdescription>
      Examples of vectors shown with their initial and terminal points.
    </shortdescription>
  </image>
  <caption>
    Examples of vectors shown with their initial and terminal points.
  </caption>
</figure>

<p>
Notice that the vector
<math>\vec{u} = \overrightarrow{CD}</math>
has the same length and the same direction as
<math>\vec{v}</math>,
even though it is in a different position.
We say that <math>\vec{u}</math> and <math>\vec{v}</math> are
<em>equivalent</em> (or <em>equal</em>), and we write
<math>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</math>.
</p>

<p>
To summarize,
<math>\overrightarrow{AB} = \overrightarrow{CD}</math>
because they have the same length and the same direction.
However,
<math>\overrightarrow{AB} \neq \overrightarrow{EF}</math>
and
<math>\overrightarrow{AB} \neq \overrightarrow{RQ}</math>
because even though they have the same length, they do not have the same direction.
</p>

<p>
The <term>zero vector</term>, denoted by
<math>\vec{0}</math>,
has length 0.
It is the only vector with no specific direction.
</p>

      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="sec-01-02-concept-check">
        <title>Concept Check</title>
        <handout>
        <example>
  <statement>
    Suppose vectors <math>\vec{u}</math> and <math>\vec{v}</math> have the same magnitude.
  </statement>

  <tasks>
    <task>
      Is this enough to guarantee that
      <math>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</math>?
      Explain why or why not.
    </task>

    <task>
      Suppose <math>\vec{u}</math> and <math>\vec{v}</math> point in the same direction.
      Is this enough to guarantee that
      <math>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</math>?
    </task>

    <task>
      State exactly what conditions must be met for two vectors to be equivalent.
      Do not draw.
      Explain your answer in words.
    </task>
  </tasks>
</example>

        </handout>
      </subsection>
